WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
Containers often receive configuration through environment variables, mounted files,

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.980
orchestration settings, or external configuration services.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.160
Secrets may include API keys, database passwords, certificates, tokens,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.300
and application credentials.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.680
The central rule is simple: secrets should not be baked into container images.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.460
Images may be stored, copied, cached, scanned, shared,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.200
or reused long after the original deployment context changes.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.220
Secret delivery should be separate from image construction.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.000
Runtime secret mounts, managed secret stores, short-lived credentials,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.740
and rotation workflows are more durable patterns than hardcoded values.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.080
Secret rotation concepts should be planned before an incident.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.020
If a key leaks, teams need to know which workloads use it, how to replace it,

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.120
how to restart or reload affected containers,

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.140
and how to verify that old credentials no longer work.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.600
Logs are another data protection concern.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.860
Applications can accidentally write passwords, tokens, request bodies, connection strings,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.800
or sensitive customer data to stdout, stderr, or sidecar logging paths.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.980
Container platforms make log collection convenient,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.280
which means sensitive data can spread quickly into centralized logging systems.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.460
Logging should preserve operational value without turning every log pipeline into a secret

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.800
repository.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.180
Persistent data needs its own design.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.560
Volumes, backups, snapshots, and database connections may outlive individual containers.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.540
Encryption at rest and in transit, at a high level,

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.520
protects data when stored and when moving across networks.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.140
Configuration should be separated from images so the same artifact can move between

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.000
environments safely while environment-specific secrets and settings remain controlled.
